Z||000000||zeusrekning||01-10-2001||09:32 AM||||What is the diff. between the GEN I and Gen II 3.8? I've seen both and know the visible differences. Dad wants carbed , just to keep it simple but if fuel injected isn't much harder we'll go that route.<BR>Can a gen II be carbed? What is the guestimated hp for a carbed v6 otherwise stock. Will bolt to my isuzu trans?||||reg||1
Z||000001||elfiero||01-10-2001||09:52 AM||||1.the 3.8 was produced until 1987<BR>2.the 3800 gen 1 was produced until 1991<BR>3.the 3800 gen II is still in production,<BR>with the 1991-1995 models being OBD I,and<BR>the 1996-2001 being OBD II<BR>4.NONE of the transverse model buick V6<BR>engines EVER came with a carb,and nobody<BR>makes an intake manifold to do it.so,if you<BR>want a 3800,it's gonna be fuel injected!||||reg||6
Z||000002||zeusrekning||01-10-2001||10:14 AM||||So we can't put a carb on a 3800? Well i guess you answered that huh,, <BR>Well what is the diff, is a 3.8 a totaly different engine that a 3800 , or some design differences. Are internal parts interchangeable?Seems like the gen II internal parts would be stronger.||||reg||1
Z||000003||elfiero||01-10-2001||11:03 AM||||the 3800 is very different from the 3.8,the<BR>the diff between the GEN I&II 3800's is as follows:<BR>GEN I has aluminum intake&valve covers,and<BR>a slightly smaller MAF sensor.<BR>GEN II has composite intake&valve covers,a<BR>larger MAF sensor,a faster baud rate processor,slightly different sensors.<BR>in 1996 GM went to OBD II,this didnt really<BR>change the powertrain per se,but after that<BR>you need an expensive scanner to do anything<BR>other than change oil!||||reg||6
Z||000004||terryk||01-10-2001||11:42 AM||||Well, the 82-85 3.0L V6's had carbs. Rochester Vari-Jet.<P>Buick was all over the map in the configurations of the different engines so it gets hard match parts from various versions.||||reg||1
